Poster Presentation Guidelines
Poster presentations will be live on Zoom meetings via the Engagez platform. Please prepare your poster so that you can screen-share it on Zoom.
Poster sessions are an excellent format for authors to present their papers and interact with conference attendees for in-depth technical discussions. The poster should be more of an introduction to the paper so as to initiate a conversation that can get into greater details.

For your virtual poster, please include short bullets highlighting:
the challenge
the approach
the experiment setup
the results
Your goal as a poster presenter in a virtual environment is to:
draw in the audience rather than overwhelm with too much text and graphics
help each attendee get a quick understanding of what your work is about
entice each attendee to engage in a conversation with the poster author (breakout rooms will be established for this purpose). If such a conversation is initiated, the poster should also include intuitively readable graphs that support the results so that the poster aids in the conversation.
encourage the audience to read your paper and follow-up with questions either by email, phone or zoom
With the virtual venue, we use Zoom Meetings for all of our sessions, and we take advantage of Breakout Rooms in zoom to accommodate the poster sessions. Attendees come to the Zoom Session and move between Breakout Rooms to talk to each of the poster presenters. The intent is for the session to be very interactive between the poster presenter(s) and the attendees.

We suggest preparing either 4-6 slides or a digital poster that provide graphics and material for discussing your paper. To prepare a digital poster, we have seen success using PowerPoint, Google Slides, or Keynote to create a very large slide. The size of the very large slide can be set in Page Layout, which is usually in the File menu. We make a single slide 3ft x 4ft (or something similar like 100 cm x 130 cm). Then when we present the digital poster in the Breakout Session, we can zoom in and out of sections of the digital poster as the discussion progresses between different aspects of the paper.

Dear HPEC Authors,

Thanks so much for your participation in HPEC and congratulations on being accepted to have your paper appear in the archival IEEE Xplore proceedings.  GenAI is rapidly changing our field and we are all doing our best to adapt.  We wanted to remind of you of the current GenAI guidance so you can make corresponding changes in your paper revisions.

IEEE requires:

“The use of content generated by artificial intelligence (AI) in an article (including but not limited to text, figures, images, and code) shall be disclosed in the acknowledgments section of any article submitted to an IEEE publication. The AI system used shall be identified, and specific sections of the article that use AI-generated content shall be identified and accompanied by a brief explanation regarding the level at which the AI system was used to generate the content."

Specifically, we are looking for acknowledgements along the lines of:

"ChatGPT vX.Y was used to produce an initial draft of Section IV Table 2 that was subsequently significantly revised."

The GenAI acknowledgments we have seen so far have not met this requirement.  Please update your GenAI acknowledgments accordingly.

Furthermore, IEEE HPEC strives for a higher standard “HPEC uses 100% human reviewers and we expect 100% human authorship", but we accept that some papers may not meet this bar at this time because of the rapidly changing environment.

Throughout the HPEC review process, the HPEC reviewers have noticed that even the most advanced GenAI tools create irrelevant or non-sequitur text that is readily apparent to an expert in the field.  It is an absolute requirement that an author must understand and be able to defend *everything* in the paper.  Anything that does not meet this requirement should be removed from the paper.  The result will be papers that are clearer and more concise. In addition, the purpose of research is to advance the frontier of human knowledge. Discussion of prior work is essential for defining this frontier.  GenAI produced literature reviews often create something more akin to an annotated bibliography (multiple sentences per reference) than a review of the relevant research (multiple references per sentence).  Please update literature reviews of prior work accordingly.

Best regards, 
Albert and Jeremy

IEEE HPEC Co-Chairs

